meta {
  name: Search products by name
  type: http
  seq: 13
}

get {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/api/products/search?q={{searchQuery}}&cursor={{searchCursor}}
  body: none
  auth: bearer
}

params:query {
  q: {{searchQuery}}
  cursor: {{searchCursor}}
}

auth:bearer {
  token: {{accessToken}}
}

docs {
  TICKET-P6-008 Slice B — global name search over the whole catalogue (both PACKAGED and
  CUSTOM products). `requireAuth` only (anonymous callers are allowed), sits behind `userLimiter`.

  Response shape: { results: [{ id, kind, name, brand, image, unverified, similarity }], nextCursor }.
  - `similarity` is the pg_trgm score (0–1) of the product's `name` against `q`. P6-007's
    "Did you mean …?" gate reads this field — it is part of the contract, not debug output.
  - `image` is already resolved to a usable URL (resolveImageUrl), like every other product read.
  - Results are ranked by similarity desc, tie-broken by rating count desc, then id — cursor
    pagination (opaque, 20 per page), never OFFSET. `nextCursor: null` means this is the last page.

  Matching: Postgres `pg_trgm`'s `%` operator against `name` and `brand` (GIN trigram indexes on
  both — see the `product_search_trgm` migration), which respects the session's
  `pg_trgm.similarity_threshold` GUC (default 0.3). That default is intentionally typo-tolerant:
  similarity('nutela', 'nutella') ≈ 0.667, well above the 0.3 floor, so `searchQuery: nutela`
  above finds "Nutella". The returned `similarity` value is always the name-vs-query score, even
  when a row only matched via its `brand`.

  Visibility (same rule as every other product read, via services/productVisibility.ts):
  - REJECTED products are excluded entirely — never returned, at any page.
  - PENDING_REVIEW products ARE returned, flagged unverified: true, for every caller including
    anonymous ones (matches GET /api/products/:productId).

  Input hygiene: `q` is capped at 100 raw characters (measured BEFORE trimming) and must be at
  least 2 characters after trimming; `cursor`, if present, must decode to this exact `q` or it's
  rejected. Both violations answer 400 { code, message } — try `searchQuery: a` or an empty
  `searchCursor` value copied from a different query to see the 400s.

  To page: copy the response's `nextCursor` string into the `searchCursor` environment variable
  and re-send.

  Out of scope here: Open Food Facts name search (local catalogue only — see FEATURES.md
  TICKET-P6-008).

  Errors: 400 (q too short/long, malformed or mismatched cursor), 401 (no/invalid session).
}
